Q: Why Getters and Setters Required ?
---------------------------------------------
-->  Because Spring (uses Jackson) accesses fields through getters and setters to convert JSON ↔ Java objects.

1. Setters → used when JSON → RequestDTO (reading request data)

2. Getters → used when ResponseDTO → JSON (sending response data)

Note: 👉 Without them, data cannot be read or written, so fields stay null or responses become empty.

Interview one-liner: Getters and setters are required because Spring uses them for serialization and deserialization of DTOs.
-------------------
in details : Think like this 👇
-------------
--> Spring cannot directly read or write your variables.
--> It uses methods.

Example
JSON coming from client
{
  "name": "Ajay"
}

Your DTO
class UserRequestDTO {
    private String name;
}
--> ❌ Spring cannot put "Ajay" into name directly.

--> What Spring actually does internally

Spring (Jackson) does this: dto.setName("Ajay");   // uses SETTER

Note: 👉 No setter = no value set = name is null

When sending response back
return dto;

Spring does: dto.getName();   // uses GETTER

Note: 👉 No getter = no value in JSON response

Very Simple Rule ✅

| Direction              | Needed     |
| ---------------------- | ---------- |
| JSON → Java (Request)  | **Setter** |
| Java → JSON (Response) | **Getter** |

Q:Can i use APIResponse<T> instead of requestDto and ResponseDto ?
--> ❌ NO — you should not use APIResponse<T> instead of RequestDTO and ResponseDTO.
--> They serve different purposes.

Very Simple Explanation (No jargon)

🔹 RequestDTO : 👉 Used to receive data from client

🔹 ResponseDTO : 👉 Used to send business data to client

🔹 APIResponse<T> : 👉 Used to wrap the response, not replace DTOs

Think Like This 🧠

🎁 APIResponse = gift box
📦 ResponseDTO = item inside box
📝 RequestDTO = order form

You can’t send an order form inside a gift box 😉

Correct Industry Usage ✅: 

1️⃣ APIResponse Wrapper (Common for all APIs) : 

public class APIResponse<T> {

    private boolean success;
    private String message;
    private T data;

    public APIResponse(boolean success, String message, T data) {
        this.success = success;
        this.message = message;
        this.data = data;
    }

    public boolean isSuccess() { return success; }
    public String getMessage() { return message; }
    public T getData() { return data; }
}

2️⃣ Entity : 

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;
    private String email;
}

3️⃣ DTOs : 

public class UserRequestDTO {
    private String name;
    private String email;
}

public class UserResponseDTO {
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private String email;
}

4️⃣ Repository : 

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
}

5️⃣ Service : 

public interface UserService {
    UserResponseDTO create(UserRequestDTO dto);
    UserResponseDTO getById(Long id);
    List<UserResponseDTO> getAll();
    UserResponseDTO update(Long id, UserRequestDTO dto);
    void delete(Long id);
}

Service Impl
-------------

@Service
public class UserServiceImpl implements UserService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository repo;

    @Override
    public UserResponseDTO create(UserRequestDTO dto) {
        User user = new User();
        user.setName(dto.getName());
        user.setEmail(dto.getEmail());
        return toDTO(repo.save(user));
    }

    @Override
    public UserResponseDTO getById(Long id) {
        User user = repo.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new RuntimeException("User not found"));
        return toDTO(user);
    }

    @Override
    public List<UserResponseDTO> getAll() {
        return repo.findAll().stream()
                .map(this::toDTO)
                .toList();
    }

    @Override
    public UserResponseDTO update(Long id, UserRequestDTO dto) {
        User user = repo.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new RuntimeException("User not found"));
        user.setName(dto.getName());
        user.setEmail(dto.getEmail());
        return toDTO(repo.save(user));
    }

    @Override
    public void delete(Long id) {
        repo.deleteById(id);
    }

    private UserResponseDTO toDTO(User user) {
        UserResponseDTO dto = new UserResponseDTO();
        dto.setId(user.getId());
        dto.setName(user.getName());
        dto.setEmail(user.getEmail());
        return dto;
    }
}


6️⃣ Controller (CRUD with APIResponse)

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
public class UserController {

    @Autowired
    private UserService service;

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<APIResponse<UserResponseDTO>> create(
            @RequestBody UserRequestDTO dto) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED)
                .body(new APIResponse<>(true, "User created", service.create(dto)));
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<APIResponse<UserResponseDTO>> getById(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
                new APIResponse<>(true, "User found", service.getById(id)));
    }

    @GetMapping
    public ResponseEntity<APIResponse<List<UserResponseDTO>>> getAll() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
                new APIResponse<>(true, "Users fetched", service.getAll()));
    }

    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<APIResponse<UserResponseDTO>> update(
            @PathVariable Long id,
            @RequestBody UserRequestDTO dto) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
                new APIResponse<>(true, "User updated", service.update(id, dto)));
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<APIResponse<Void>> delete(@PathVariable Long id) {
        service.delete(id);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
                new APIResponse<>(true, "User deleted", null));
    }
}
Note: 
✅ There is NO change in the Service layer

When you introduce APIResponse<T>:
--> 👉 Service layer stays exactly the same
--> 👉 Only the Controller layer changes

Why? (Simple words) : 
--> Service layer → business logic (create, update, delete, fetch)
--> APIResponse<T> → only for HTTP response formatting
--> Business logic must not depend on API structure
